# AWS Compute & Containerization Services

A practical guide to understanding and choosing AWS compute and containerization services:

* Amazon EC2
* AWS Lambda
* AWS Fargate
* AWS Elastic Beanstalk
* Amazon ECS
* Amazon EKS
* Amazon ECR

The goal of this repository is to understand **what each service does, when to use it, and how these services fit together in a modern AWS architecture.**

---

## ☁️ AWS Compute Landscape

AWS provides multiple ways to run applications, ranging from fully managed serverless services to complete infrastructure control.

```text
                    AWS COMPUTE
                         │
          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
          │              │              │
       Servers       Serverless      Containers
          │              │              │
        EC2           Lambda       ┌──────┴──────┐
                                   │             │
                                  ECS           EKS
                                   │             │
                                Fargate       Fargate/EC2
```

---

# 1. Amazon EC2

**Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2)** provides resizable virtual servers in AWS.

### Key characteristics

* Full control over the operating system
* Choose CPU, memory, storage and networking
* Supports Linux and Windows
* Supports Auto Scaling
* Integrates with Elastic Load Balancing
* Can run Docker and Kubernetes workloads
* Pay for the compute capacity you provision

### Use cases

* Traditional web applications
* Legacy applications
* Custom software environments
* High-control workloads
* Databases and specialized applications
* Applications requiring specific OS configurations

### Example

```text
Internet
   │
   ▼
Load Balancer
   │
   ▼
Auto Scaling Group
   │
 ┌─┴─────────┐
 ▼           ▼
EC2         EC2
Instance    Instance
```

### Think of EC2 as:

> "I want a virtual server and I want control over it."

---

# 2. AWS Lambda

AWS Lambda is a **serverless, event-driven compute service**.

You upload your code and AWS manages the underlying infrastructure.

### Key characteristics

* No server management
* Event-driven
* Automatic scaling
* Pay based on usage
* Supports multiple programming languages
* Integrates with many AWS services

### Common triggers

```text
API Gateway
     │
     ▼
   Lambda
     │
 ┌───┼────┐
 ▼   ▼    ▼
S3  SQS  DynamoDB
```

### Use cases

* REST APIs
* File processing
* Automation
* Scheduled tasks
* Event processing
* Backend functions

### Think of Lambda as:

> "Run my code when something happens, without managing servers."

---

# 3. AWS Fargate

AWS Fargate is a **serverless compute engine for containers**.

Instead of managing EC2 instances for your containers, AWS manages the underlying compute infrastructure.

Fargate works with:

* Amazon ECS
* Amazon EKS

### Architecture

```text
Docker Image
     │
     ▼
    ECR
     │
     ▼
ECS / EKS
     │
     ▼
  Fargate
     │
     ▼
Running Container
```

### Benefits

* No EC2 server management
* Automatic infrastructure management
* Container-based deployment
* Resource-based pricing
* Works well with microservices

### Think of Fargate as:

> "I want containers, but I don't want to manage the servers running them."

---

# 4. AWS Elastic Beanstalk

Elastic Beanstalk is a **managed application deployment service**.

You provide your application code, and Beanstalk handles much of the infrastructure setup.

It can provision and manage resources such as:

* EC2
* Load Balancer
* Auto Scaling
* Security Groups
* CloudWatch integration

### Typical architecture

```text
Developer
    │
    ▼
Application Code
    │
    ▼
Elastic Beanstalk
    │
 ┌──┼──────────┐
 ▼  ▼          ▼
EC2 ALB   Auto Scaling
```

### Use cases

* Quickly deploying web applications
* Developers who don't want to configure infrastructure manually
* Simple production applications
* Rapid application environments

### Think of Elastic Beanstalk as:

> "I have application code and want AWS to handle much of the infrastructure setup."

---

# 5. Amazon ECS

Amazon Elastic Container Service is an AWS-native **container orchestration service**.

ECS can run containers using:

* EC2
* Fargate

### Architecture

```text
              ECS Cluster
                   │
          ┌────────┴────────┐
          │                 │
       Service           Service
          │                 │
     ┌────┴────┐       ┌────┴────┐
     ▼         ▼       ▼         ▼
 Container Container Container Container
```

### ECS with EC2

You manage the underlying EC2 instances.

```text
ECS
 │
 ▼
EC2 Instances
 │
 ▼
Containers
```

### ECS with Fargate

AWS manages the underlying compute infrastructure.

```text
ECS
 │
 ▼
Fargate
 │
 ▼
Containers
```

### Use cases

* Microservices
* Docker workloads
* APIs
* Web applications
* Batch processing

### Think of ECS as:

> "I want AWS-native container orchestration."

---

# 6. Amazon EKS

Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service is AWS's **managed Kubernetes service**.

EKS manages the Kubernetes control plane while you choose how to run your worker workloads.

### Architecture

```text
                 EKS
                  │
          Kubernetes Control Plane
                  │
        ┌─────────┴─────────┐
        │                   │
      EC2                Fargate
        │                   │
        ▼                   ▼
     Pods                  Pods
```

### Key features

* Managed Kubernetes control plane
* Kubernetes API compatibility
* High availability
* Integration with AWS networking and security
* Supports Kubernetes ecosystem tools
* Can run workloads on EC2 or Fargate

### Use cases

* Kubernetes-based applications
* Complex microservices
* Multi-cloud Kubernetes strategies
* Organizations already using Kubernetes
* Advanced container orchestration

### Think of EKS as:

> "I need Kubernetes on AWS."

---

# 7. Amazon ECR

Amazon Elastic Container Registry is a **managed container image registry**.

It stores Docker/OCI container images.

### Workflow

```text
Developer
    │
    ▼
Docker Build
    │
    ▼
Docker Image
    │
    ▼
   ECR
    │
    ├──────────────┐
    ▼              ▼
   ECS            EKS
    │              │
    ▼              ▼
Container        Pod
```

### Common commands

```bash
# Authenticate Docker with ECR
aws ecr get-login-password --region <region> | \
docker login --username AWS --password-stdin <account>.dkr.ecr.<region>.amazonaws.com

# Build image
docker build -t my-app .

# Tag image
docker tag my-app:latest <repository-url>:latest

# Push image
docker push <repository-url>:latest
```

### Use cases

* Store Docker images
* CI/CD pipelines
* ECS deployments
* EKS deployments
* Image version management

### Think of ECR as:

> "A private AWS registry for my container images."

